From a4bc7040a1f08ffb252d2bcd702cf89167bb156b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Braden <snufflyy@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 23:22:13 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] cellOvis: implement the real overlay-table API

The module exported invented functions (cellOvisInit/Term/CreateOverlay/...)
and a cellOvisGetOverlayTableSize(path, u32*) that wrote its result through
the caller's r4 and returned NOT_INITIALIZED, since no title calls the
invented Init. A title that sizes and allocates the table from the return
value then asks for ~2 GB and gives up ("Error allocating memory for overlay
table"), and cellOvisInitializeOverlayTable (NID 0xA876C911) was not
registered at all, so it faked CELL_OK and left the table uninitialised.

libovis serves SPU ELFs linked with overlays: several PT_LOADs share one
local-store range and each overlay's p_paddr is a load address outside LS.
The SPU-side manager DMA-GETs an overlay from table + p_paddr, so:

  cellOvisGetOverlayTableSize(elf EA)  -> highest overlay p_paddr + p_memsz,
                                          rounded to 128; INVAL if the ELF has
                                          no overlay segment (callers skip it)
  cellOvisInitializeOverlayTable(t, elf) -> zero t, copy each overlay's file
                                          bytes to t + p_paddr; ALIGN unless t
                                          is 128-byte aligned

Overlay segments are recognised by a p_paddr outside local store (>= 256 KiB)
that differs from p_vaddr (resident ones have p_paddr == p_vaddr or 0).
Reconstructed from a
PhysX title (SARPBC): its PPU loop treats 0/INVAL as "no overlays", passes
the table pointer verbatim to the SPU task, and the SPU manager reads
{vma, size, lma, loaded} entries matching the ELF's overlay p_vaddr/p_paddr
and DMAs from table + lma. FixSpuSegments / InvalidateOverlappedSegments are
not implemented yet (no importer seen).

The registration table must be regenerated (gen_hle_nids.py --all): it
still names the removed functions.

Test: tests/test_ovis_overlay_table.c (synthetic SPU ELF in guest memory).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
